1\n00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,839\n(Naran)\nKamran Razmavar presents another country's argument, and Jesus did NOT meet Armenians, and in the Bible, there are NO Armenians at all, which means ancient Armenia is invented.\n\n2\n00:00:09,199 --> 00:00:12,279\n(Naran)\nBut the existence of countries and peoples is NOT established this way.\n\n3\n00:00:12,580 --> 00:00:16,359\n(Naran)\nAnd now let's dispel this myth about Greater Armenia, which allegedly included Jerusalem.\n\n4\n00:00:16,920 --> 00:00:21,500\n(Naran)\nAccording to Wikipedia, Greater Armenia therefore did not exist from the fourth century, so our raids are approximately from the fifth.\n\n5\n00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:28,019\n(Naran)\nAnd somewhere in the middle of these 80 years, he was born in Bethlehem, by the way, it's near Jerusalem. And Jesus Christ.\n\n6\n00:00:28,019 --> 00:00:36,899\n(Naran)\nStrangely enough, not a single Armenian did NOT meet Jesus. There is NO Armenian in the Bible. There is NO great Armenia, there are dozens of state nationalities and NOT ethnicities.\n\n7\n00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:41,960\n(Naran)\nBut there are NO Armenians, NO Armenia, NO need to push it there, it says 'Mountains of Ararat'. This is a geographical term.\n\n8\n00:00:42,259 --> 00:00:50,200\n(Naran)\nThe Gospel is NOT a list of all the peoples of the Middle East. The absence of a people in the story of Christ's life does NOT mean its absence in history.\n\n9\n00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:55,359\n(Naran)\nMoreover, the name of Armenia is fixed about 5 centuries before the birth of Jesus.\n\n10\n00:00:55,820 --> 00:01:00,799\n(Naran)\nAround 520 BC, Darius the First left the Behistun inscription.\n\n11\n00:01:01,179 --> 00:01:05,500\n(Naran)\nIn the Dripepersian text, the country is called 'Armina or the inhabitant of Armenia'.\n\n12\n00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:12,200\n(Naran)\nThe Encyclopedia Iranica translates the latter as Armenian. Now to Kamran's assertion that there is Ararat in the Bible, but there are NO Armenians.\n\n13\n00:01:13,060 --> 00:01:16,700\n(Naran)\nEven this is NOT true if we talk about Biblical translations in general.\n\n14\n00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:29,719\n(Naran)\nIn the classic English translation of the King James Bible, in 2 Kings 19:37 and in the book of Isaiah 37:38, the expression land of Armenia is directly used.\n\n15\n00:01:30,219 --> 00:01:34,120\n(Naran)\nIn modern translations, in these same places, the land of Ararat is more often used.\n\n16\n00:01:34,299 --> 00:01:36,700\n(Naran)\nAnd this shows the main problem of the argument to Kamran.\n\n17\n00:01:37,599 --> 00:01:43,239\n(Naran)\nDifferent linguistic and translation traditions can convey ancient geographical names differently.\n\n18\n00:01:43,239 --> 00:01:54,680\n(Naran)\nThis is also seen in the Behistun inscription. Ancient Persian versions use the name Armina, while in Velosko-Uras, which means the old Mesopotamian name 'Urartu' that therefore did not exist earlier in the same region.\n\n19\n00:01:54,939 --> 00:02:00,159\n(Naran)\nThere are independent Greek testimonies. Herodotus from the fifth century BC mentions Armenians.\n\n20\n00:02:00,359 --> 00:02:05,659\n(Naran)\nXenophon in the early 4th century BC repeatedly writes about Armenia and Armenians.\n\n21\n00:02:05,859 --> 00:02:10,960\n(Naran)\nTherefore, Kamran's question of why Jesus did NOT meet Armenians historically proves nothing.\n\n22\n00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:16,740\n(Naran)\nPersian and Greek sources record Armenia and Armenians centuries before 'the roads of Christ'.\n\n23\n00:02:16,740 --> 00:02:27,879\n(Naran)\nAnd the attempt to prove the absence of Armenia with the word 'Ararat' collapses on the sources themselves. Kamran simply mixes different linguistic traditions and takes the difference in names for the absence of the country.\n\n24\n00:02:44,079 --> 00:02:48,959\n(Other Speaker)\nlike you know where the first man was found but civilization, modern civilization.\n\n25\n00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:52,699\n(Other Speaker)\n